--TEST--
A `Core\Arr::shapeAs` conversion collects every field that did not fit into one `ParseError`, each at its own path — `rule:core-classes/derive-reports-every-field` and `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Three ways to fail in one call: a value `as` refuses, a required key that is
// absent, and a second value `as` refuses — the empty string, which is not an
// exact numeric literal either. The report is one throw carrying all of them,
// because a form needs the list rather than the first item of it.
try {
    Core\Arr::shapeAs<{a: int, b: int, c: int}>(["a" => "x", "c" => ""]);
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $bad) {
    echo "issues=", Core\Arr::count($bad->issues) as string, "\n";
    echo "  ", $bad->issues["0"]->path, ": ", $bad->issues["0"]->message, "\n";
    echo "  ", $bad->issues["1"]->path, ": ", $bad->issues["1"]->message, "\n";
    echo "  ", $bad->issues["2"]->path, ": ", $bad->issues["2"]->message, "\n";
}

// No row of `rule:types/conversion` lands on `bool` — PHP's truthy table is a
// condition's answer and not a conversion's — so a checkbox's `"1"` is a
// failed field rather than a `true` this member invented.
try {
    Core\Arr::shapeAs<{flag: bool}>(["flag" => "1"]);
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $closed) {
    echo $closed->issues["0"]->path, ": ", $closed->issues["0"]->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
issues=3
  a: expected int, found a string
  b: required field missing
  c: expected int, found a string
flag: expected bool, found a string
